<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    bg_bubble_received — background for received message bubbles
    ===
    Referenced by:
      - item_message_received.xml (root container background)

    Sibling of bg_bubble_sent.xml. The two files differ in
    exactly three attributes:

      | aspect          | bg_bubble_sent       | bg_bubble_received  |
      |===|===|===|
      | fill attribute  | pulseBubbleSent      | pulseBubbleReceived |
      | tail corner     | bottom-right (4dp)   | bottom-left  (4dp)  |
      | resolved light  | #6D5EF5 (indigo)     | #F1EEF6 (light gray)|
      | resolved dark   | #4E42D1 (deep indigo)| #2A2831 (dark gray) |

    Every other attribute is identical: rounded corners (18dp)
    on the three non-tail corners, no stroke, no gradient, no
    <size> element. If you ever change one of the shared
    properties, change both files in the same commit.

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. THEME ATTRIBUTE FILL, NOT COLOR RESOURCE.
       ?attr/pulseBubbleReceived resolves through the active
       theme:
         - light theme → @color/bubble_received_light (#F1EEF6)
         - dark theme  → @color/bubble_received_dark (#2A2831)
       One drawable file, both themes. Do NOT hardcode either
       color value here.

    2. TAIL CORNER AT BOTTOM-LEFT.
       Received bubbles are left-aligned in the chat. The
       small-radius corner is therefore bottom-LEFT — the
       side facing the screen edge on the receiver's side.
       This is the directional mirror of the sent bubble,
       and the two bubbles visually "point" toward their
       respective senders.

       Do NOT put the tail on the bottom-right for this file.
       A received message with a bottom-right tail would
       look like it is "about to be sent" instead of
       "received." The whole visual distinction between
       sent and received bubbles is:
         - alignment (right vs. left)
         - color (saturated vs. neutral)
         - tail corner (bottom-right vs. bottom-left)
       Getting any of these three wrong flips the bubble's
       meaning.

    3. NEUTRAL COLOR.
       Sent bubbles use a saturated brand color (indigo) so
       that the user's own messages visually "pop." Received
       bubbles use a neutral surface-variant color (light
       gray in light mode, dark gray in dark mode) so they
       recede — the user's eye is naturally drawn to their
       own messages first when scanning a conversation.
       This is the standard messaging color convention used
       by WhatsApp, Telegram, iMessage, and every other
       messaging app. Do NOT make received bubbles saturated
       (e.g., a second brand color) — that would produce a
       visually noisy conversation where every message
       competes for attention.

    4. NO STROKE, NO GRADIENT, NO SHADOW.
       Same rationale as bg_bubble_sent.xml: flat-filled,
       contrast-by-color-not-by-decoration. Do not add
       embellishments here.

    No <size> element is declared. Shape drawables size to
    their consumer View's bounds — adding <size> would
    force an intrinsic width that the view would have to
    override, producing inconsistent bubble widths. Omit
    <size> and let the view drive the shape's dimensions.
    ============================================================
-->
<shape
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">

    <solid android:color="?attr/pulseBubbleReceived" />

    <corners
        android:topLeftRadius="@dimen/bubble_radius"
        android:topRightRadius="@dimen/bubble_radius"
        android:bottomLeftRadius="@dimen/bubble_tail_radius"
        android:bottomRightRadius="@dimen/bubble_radius" />

</shape>
